WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.560
So, what's the way forward after the murder of George Floyd?

00:00:05.560 --> 00:00:07.480
That's the question I'd like to get into today.

00:00:07.480 --> 00:00:12.000
My name is Brett Silford, pastor at The Table, a church in Sac State, Texas, and welcome

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:16.559
to Table Talk, where each week I unpack ideas and insights at the intersection of Christian

00:00:16.559 --> 00:00:18.040
theology and culture.

00:00:18.040 --> 00:00:21.280
Alright, let's get into our conversation today.

00:00:21.280 --> 00:00:27.440
So nearly all of you have seen the video of George Floyd being brutally murdered by the

00:00:27.440 --> 00:00:33.680
white police officer, and it rightly sparked outrage across the political spectrum.

00:00:33.680 --> 00:00:37.959
It was a disgusting act of systemic racism.

00:00:37.959 --> 00:00:41.720
Now why do I say systemic and not simply racism?

00:00:41.720 --> 00:00:49.759
Because as a uniformed officer, he is part of a broader culture, a system, a network

00:00:49.759 --> 00:00:54.759
that too often is engaged in acts like this.

00:00:54.759 --> 00:00:59.200
And now, somewhat predictably, the peaceful protests have, by a much smaller subset of

00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:04.220
people, at times turned violent, though honestly I'm really only shocked that the violence

00:01:04.220 --> 00:01:07.760
hasn't been worse.

00:01:07.760 --> 00:01:13.400
And I should also add, it's not entirely clear who exactly is instigating the violence.

00:01:13.400 --> 00:01:15.980
Now having said that, the question is where do we go from here?

00:01:15.980 --> 00:01:22.639
So I thought Van Jones, from CNN, summed it up really beautifully when he said, and here

00:01:22.639 --> 00:01:28.199
I'm going to paraphrase, we get to choose whether this summer we're going to have a

00:01:28.199 --> 00:01:31.000
breakthrough or a breakdown.

00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:37.000
He said, if we get bipartisan police reform and have these four officers charged, then

00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:38.639
we'll get a breakthrough.

00:01:38.639 --> 00:01:45.779
If not, we're one video away from civil unrest and a breakdown.

00:01:45.779 --> 00:01:49.120
So what does Van Jones mean by police reform?

00:01:49.120 --> 00:01:57.559
Well he's advocating for the implementation of practices and procedures done in places

00:01:57.559 --> 00:02:01.879
all across the country, but in a place, for example, like Richmond, California, has been

00:02:01.879 --> 00:02:03.000
a great success story.

00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:10.360
So according to richmondpulse.org, there's a story there that I'll link to, that in Richmond,

00:02:10.360 --> 00:02:15.279
the police chief there has done a number of different things, right, because it's not

00:02:15.279 --> 00:02:16.279
just one thing.

00:02:16.279 --> 00:02:24.000
He has shifted the emphasis from an enforcement-driven, military-style kind of ethos and culture that

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:30.039
says we're going to eradicate crime by ratcheting up the level of force that we use, to one

00:02:30.039 --> 00:02:36.479
that says we're going to begin building as much trust with our community as possible,

00:02:36.479 --> 00:02:42.679
especially the non-white community, because their trust is basically gone.

00:02:42.679 --> 00:02:48.039
In the words of police chief Chris Magnus, quote, the biggest challenge has been changing

00:02:48.039 --> 00:02:53.000
the perception black community members have toward the police, those who have experienced

00:02:53.000 --> 00:02:56.639
a legacy of mistrust.

00:02:56.639 --> 00:03:01.039
So how did this police chief, how did he do this?

00:03:01.039 --> 00:03:03.240
Well it wasn't, of course, as I said, just one thing.

00:03:03.240 --> 00:03:04.520
It was a number of things.

00:03:04.520 --> 00:03:09.600
He began promoting officers based on merit and performance rather than relational connections

00:03:09.600 --> 00:03:12.080
or simply seniority.

00:03:12.080 --> 00:03:17.759
It meant he demoted and fired more officers in his short tenure than in the last 20 years

00:03:17.759 --> 00:03:18.759
combined.

00:03:18.759 --> 00:03:23.839
It meant having the police officers assigned to specific neighborhoods for their careers

00:03:23.839 --> 00:03:28.919
so they could form long-term relationships with the people there and develop a reputation,

00:03:28.919 --> 00:03:33.880
right, become known, as opposed to rotating through different areas each year.

00:03:33.880 --> 00:03:38.559
It meant the officers became a fixture at city meetings, neighborhood events, volunteer

00:03:38.559 --> 00:03:39.559
opportunities.

00:03:39.559 --> 00:03:44.520
It meant doing less time in the police cruiser, more time walking around, meeting people.

00:03:44.520 --> 00:03:49.720
In fact, an officer named Yesenia Rogers, who was part of this department in Richmond

00:03:49.720 --> 00:03:54.839
undergoing these reforms, she actually chose to give her personal cell number to business

00:03:54.839 --> 00:04:00.360
owners, to residents, so they could contact her if they saw any illegal activity.

00:04:00.360 --> 00:04:07.080
And finally, it meant intentionally shifting from older-style force to preventative policing

00:04:07.080 --> 00:04:13.160
and verbal de-escalating, which has led to a strong decrease in the use of handcuffs,

00:04:13.160 --> 00:04:15.960
batons, guns, etc.

00:04:15.960 --> 00:04:22.040
Resident and volunteer Benny Louie Singleton, she said that she used to be afraid to even

00:04:22.040 --> 00:04:27.079
drive down certain streets, that she now walks without fear.

00:04:27.079 --> 00:04:32.959
And this is her quote, this is all because the community has started interacting with

00:04:32.959 --> 00:04:34.920
police and vice versa.

00:04:34.920 --> 00:04:39.079
I've never been so comfortable with police, she said.

00:04:39.079 --> 00:04:43.200
It's like everything else, when you get to know a person, you look at them differently.

00:04:43.200 --> 00:04:49.059
So it's changes like these, and many others, that we have to begin implementing to change

00:04:49.059 --> 00:04:55.239
the culture in policing, so that deaths like Floyd's become a thing of the past.

00:04:55.239 --> 00:05:00.640
And I suspect that these and other changes that increase the level of police accountability

00:05:00.640 --> 00:05:04.480
are efforts that the majority of officers who are simply trying to keep their communities

00:05:04.480 --> 00:05:07.040
safe would welcome.

00:05:07.040 --> 00:05:12.559
Things like transparency, trust, and accountability, of course, have to become the new normal.

00:05:12.559 --> 00:05:19.119
Now, you might say that these protests aren't necessary, they're just causing trouble, they're

00:05:19.119 --> 00:05:20.519
all for nothing.

00:05:20.519 --> 00:05:22.600
I don't think that's accurate at all.

00:05:22.600 --> 00:05:27.160
The purpose of protests is pressure and attention.

00:05:27.160 --> 00:05:31.920
It's keeping a thing in front of us and in front of our leaders long enough to do something

00:05:31.920 --> 00:05:34.239
about it.

00:05:35.000 --> 00:05:40.359
So to read more about police reform in Richmond, like I said, I'll link that article below

00:05:40.359 --> 00:05:44.160
and it will give you more of the story.

00:05:44.160 --> 00:05:46.980
But that's kind of the call to action, I guess.

00:05:46.980 --> 00:05:54.640
Like Van Jones said, we have a decision before us, breakthrough or breakdown, and I'm pushing

00:05:54.640 --> 00:05:58.880
for a breakthrough, and I hope you'll join me.

00:05:58.880 --> 00:05:59.880
So that's all for today.

00:06:00.119 --> 00:06:04.959
If you enjoyed this video, please share it, comment below, and to ensure you don't miss

00:06:04.959 --> 00:06:11.040
future episodes, you can subscribe to my YouTube channel or podcast by searching Brett Tilford

00:06:11.040 --> 00:06:12.040
TableTalk.

00:06:12.040 --> 00:06:13.480
Grace and peace.

